{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head 2d74a93f5698bb6942fa2eb46e98d10e80a783e5, artifact 3ccb2a46, against 967e62b6. One file, notes/2026-08-27-browser-playability.md, 266 lines added and nothing else touched.\n\nDECIDABLE, which was my main test since I asked for a design rather than an implementation. Yes. The load-bearing choices are pinned and each is refutable. Key custody is named exactly: both CryptoKey objects in one IndexedDB record keyed by repository genesis and a local format version, extractable false. Encoding ownership is settled in the direction I hoped for and stated flatly: the browser does not reimplement canonical JSON, Git object hashing or deterministic CBOR, and signs the exact byte string the host-backed prepare returns. I checked the intent layout the note reproduces against the core module rather than taking it: internal/intent/intent.go carries domainTag \"gitseq.intent.v0\\x00\" and an Intent struct tagged cbor:\",toarray\" with exactly the nine fields in the order the note gives. A wrong build would be visible: a browser-side encoder, a per-game key, a CORS grant, a server-held player key, or a parity fixture without tamper cases would each contradict a specific sentence.\n\nNO SERVER-HELD PLAYER KEY. Holds, and delegation is refused with a reason rather than omitted: delegation would make the process able to forge every move made through it. The only server key remains the sequencer key already sitting in the repository, and the note is careful that a sequencer signature cannot stand in for a browser actor signature. That distinction is ordering authority versus authorship and the note keeps it.\n\nKEY LOSS. Holds, and this is where I expected to find the gap. The note states the hard fact rather than engineering around it: loss of an unanchored key before an identity upgrade is final, no remaining key can act for that seat, the game may be unable to finish, and the UI must say so. It explicitly refuses to hide that behind a server recovery key. The anchored-recovery path it describes is the behaviour chess already implements, and it forbids silently replacing a missing key while presenting the new fingerprint as the old player, which is the failure that would have made the UI lie about identity.\n\nSIGNED RELAY. Holds in substance, with one caveat I want on the record rather than buried. The server never holds a signing key and never alters a signed intent; submission recomputes the structure and compares before anything is written, and a changed move reusing a signature is refused before append. But the server does construct the bytes the player signs, and the browser signs them opaquely, verifying only the human-readable echo. An actively malicious serve process could therefore obtain a signature over bytes of its choosing. I am not treating that as a defect, because the same process serves the page's script: there is no arrangement in this deployment model where a malicious server lacks that power, and the alternative the note rejects, a browser-side intent encoder, would introduce the second canonical encoder this design exists to avoid. The note says the equivalent thing itself, that a same-origin script compromise has the authority of that browser actor. I record it because a future reader should meet this as a known bound, not discover it.\n\nEXACT INTENT AND PARITY, CLI INTEROPERABILITY, AND THE FOLD BOUNDARY. All hold. The signed intent pins the payload tree, so the kernel records exactly what was signed. Seat semantics are unchanged and the note is candid about the one asymmetry it creates: a browser-joined unanchored seat can only be played by that browser key, because its non-extractable private half is not copied into a CLI key file. Interoperability runs through the existing anchoring vocabulary rather than being inferred from a display name or shared machine access, which is the right answer.\n\nTRUST AND FOLD BOUNDARIES UNCHANGED, which I said would be changes-requested if violated. No fold rule, schema, seat matching or decision rule changes. Two boundary changes exist and both are explicit rather than quiet: the serve process gains a writing workspace and therefore engages the sequencer key already beside it on disk, and a new Gitseq public-host prepare and submit API is required because the canonical intent and kernel submission types are private to that module. The note routes the second correctly, saying that Gitseq change needs its own exact-head architecture update and review before chess consumes it. That is the right instinct and it matches what I found: the only public write path today is Workspace.Append, and it refuses the binding schema by design.\n\nHONESTY. The frontmatter says draft design, no browser durable-write path is implemented, and I confirmed that is true of the head. The note names its own hardest facts rather than its best ones.\n\nTWO NON-BLOCKING CORRECTIONS for whenever this page is next touched. The scope boundary names TrustedHostHandler, which is the core repository's symbol; the chess-side guard is trustedLiveHost in cmd/chess/web.go. And one sentence in the Security conclusion, that the server cannot forge a player act, is true of a server at rest but not of an actively malicious one obtaining blind signatures; the adjacent sentence carries the correction, so the two should not be quotable apart. Neither changes the verdict.\n\nARCHITECTURE: the note itself changes no contract. The design it describes extends the Gitseq host public surface, refactors chess act builders without touching the fold, moves the chess serve composition from read-only to writing behind the existing mutation guard, and adds browser key persistence. The one genuine exported-contract extension is correctly routed to its own review in the core repository.\n\nSECURITY: no unresolved defect. Player custody moves from a memory-only tab variable to an origin-and-genesis-scoped non-extractable key that never reaches the server. A cross-origin attacker gains nothing: no CORS grant, loopback host check, Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site checks, and no server-side key to trick into signing. The residual risks are named and each is bounded by an existing mechanism, except the blind-signing bound above, which is inherent rather than introduced.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. The note is already its own simplification pass, refusing delegation, escrow, export, a JavaScript rules engine and a JavaScript intent encoder, and keeping one key for live and durable identity and one encoder per layer.\n\nGates at the exact head, in the worktree: gofmt silent, build clean, vet clean, go test ./... -count=1 with both packages ok and exit 0, as expected for a note-only head.\n\nWHAT THIS UNBLOCKS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Approving the design does not authorize implementation. The hard dependency is the core-side prepare and submit API, which does not exist; that belongs in the core workroom with its own architecture update and review before any chess implementation head can be filed. I am carrying that as the next durable request rather than leaving it implied.","body":{"head":"2d74a93f5698bb6942fa2eb46e98d10e80a783e5","verdict":"approved"}}